WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.054 --> 00:00:10.265
FAYE MOSS: Hello, everyone! Welcome back to Superstars in History , where I chat with famous figures from

2
00:00:10.266 --> 00:00:17.750
the past! Today's guest was an American president who led his nation through the tough times of the Civil

3
00:00:17.751 --> 00:00:27.187
War. You might know him as the president who abolished slavery in the U.S., but the truth is a bit more complicated than that.

4
00:00:27.188 --> 00:00:44.885
Please welcome Abraham Lincoln!

5
00:00:44.886 --> 00:00:47.477
ABRAHAM LINCOLN: Thank you for having

6
00:00:47.478 --> 00:00:50.645
me, Faye. FAYE MOSS: Thanks for joining

7
00:00:50.646 --> 00:00:56.850
us, President Lincoln! I can't wait to hear how you became one of the most famous

8
00:00:56.851 --> 00:01:00.163
U.S. presidents ever. Why don't we start with

9
00:01:00.164 --> 00:01:04.177
your childhood? ABRAHAM LINCOLN: I was born in

10
00:01:04.178 --> 00:01:09.177
1809 in Hodgenville, Kentucky. When I was seven, my father lost almost all of his

11
00:01:09.178 --> 00:01:14.343
land in lawsuits. We moved to Indiana, where there were more opportunities to buy

12
00:01:14.344 --> 00:01:18.925
new land. We arrived with very little and lived in a log cabin my

13
00:01:18.926 --> 00:01:25.155
father built. FAYE MOSS: It sounds like your father was pretty handy! What was

14
00:01:25.156 --> 00:01:30.112
your mother like? ABRAHAM LINCOLN: Sadly my mother died when I was young,

15
00:01:30.113 --> 00:01:36.518
but my father remarried. I was very close with my stepmother. She encouraged me to read and learn as

16
00:01:36.519 --> 00:01:42.145
much as I could. I didn't have the time or money to go to school, so I taught

17
00:01:42.146 --> 00:01:46.855
myself almost everything. I loved to learn, both as a child and throughout

18
00:01:46.856 --> 00:01:52.830
my life. FAYE MOSS: That's impressive! But eventually

19
00:01:52.831 --> 00:01:57.918
you left Indiana, right? ABRAHAM LINCOLN: That's true! When I was 21,

20
00:01:57.919 --> 00:02:01.393
my family moved to Illinois. I did a few odd jobs

21
00:02:01.394 --> 00:02:06.416
but didn't make much money. Eventually an acquaintance named John Todd Stuart encouraged me

22
00:02:06.417 --> 00:02:12.909
to become a lawyer. I studied the law and taught myself. Stuart hired me at his firm once I got

23
00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:19.310
my law license. People liked the way I was straightforward and fair in court, so they started calling me

24
00:02:19.311 --> 00:02:28.224
"Honest Abe." FAYE MOSS: Wow, you were self-taught in law too! You also started a family of your own

25
00:02:28.225 --> 00:02:34.227
around this time. ABRAHAM LINCOLN: Yes. I married my wife, Mary Todd, a

26
00:02:34.228 --> 00:02:39.859
cousin of John's, in 1842. Together we had four sons, but two died when

27
00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:42.962
they were only children. We were very sad when we

28
00:02:42.963 --> 00:02:50.292
lost our boys. FAYE MOSS: I'm sorry for your loss. Let's move on. How

29
00:02:50.293 --> 00:02:58.001
did you get into politics? ABRAHAM LINCOLN: While training to be a lawyer, I decided to run for the Illinois state legislature

30
00:02:58.002 --> 00:03:02.929
and was elected for several years. Eventually I spoke out against the Mexican-American War that was

31
00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:08.860
being fought at the time. This upset many voters, so I decided not to run

32
00:03:08.861 --> 00:03:13.687
for reelection after that. FAYE MOSS: The Mexican-American War wasn't the

33
00:03:13.688 --> 00:03:18.422
only big issue at the time. Slavery

34
00:03:18.423 --> 00:03:22.843
was also being hotly debated. ABRAHAM LINCOLN: Abolitionists in the North were

35
00:03:22.844 --> 00:03:27.987
calling for a complete end to slavery. This concerned the South because its economy depended

36
00:03:27.988 --> 00:03:36.007
on slaves who worked the farms. The South was also worried about the federal government growing too powerful; many thought states should

37
00:03:36.008 --> 00:03:43.676
have more power and independence. Meanwhile the U.S. was still expanding westward, and the new states had to decide if they would

38
00:03:43.677 --> 00:03:46.391
allow slavery or not. FAYE MOSS: What

39
00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:54.068
was your position on the issue? ABRAHAM LINCOLN: I had always hated slavery, but I didn't support abolition, since

40
00:03:54.069 --> 00:04:00.458
it didn't seem possible under the Constitution. In states where slavery already existed, I thought the states should be able to choose whether

41
00:04:00.459 --> 00:04:03.662
to keep it or end it. I didn't want slavery to

42
00:04:03.663 --> 00:04:09.633
expand into new states though. I started speaking out more vehemently on the issue and debated it with my opponent when I

43
00:04:09.634 --> 00:04:15.812
ran for the Senate. Although I lost the election, our debates were very popular, and I gained

44
00:04:15.813 --> 00:04:19.513
attention from voters. FAYE MOSS: Is that when you

45
00:04:19.514 --> 00:04:22.849
decided to run for president? ABRAHAM LINCOLN: Many people encouraged

46
00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:29.416
me to run, so I did. And in 1861, I became president! I'm afraid

47
00:04:29.417 --> 00:04:36.289
my victory angered the South, though. Seven Southern states joined together and declared themselves their own nation,

48
00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:43.709
the Confederate States of America. I believed leaving the United States—leaving the Union—was an illegal action, but

49
00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:46.775
I didn't want war. That's why I refused to

50
00:04:46.776 --> 00:04:53.144
make the first strike. But when a commander asked for supplies in Fort Sumter, South Carolina, I sent what

51
00:04:53.145 --> 00:04:58.846
he asked for. The South took that as an action against them, and their army started firing on

52
00:04:58.847 --> 00:05:01.427
Fort Sumter. The Civil War

53
00:05:01.428 --> 00:05:05.986
had begun. FAYE MOSS: You were president for only a few months when

54
00:05:05.987 --> 00:05:08.938
war broke out! That must

55
00:05:08.939 --> 00:05:14.786
have been tough. ABRAHAM LINCOLN: Indeed it was. I took control and

56
00:05:14.787 --> 00:05:23.088
shaped our military strategy. There was a string of commanders for my Union army, but none was able to carry out a large-scale attack on the Confederacy

57
00:05:23.089 --> 00:05:27.925
in the South. I finally found Ulysses S. Grant, whom I named general

58
00:05:27.926 --> 00:05:36.093
in chief. He was able to command the army the way I wanted. Meanwhile I was trying to figure out the future of slavery in the

59
00:05:36.094 --> 00:05:46.523
country. FAYE MOSS: We know what you decided. In 1863 you issued the Emancipation Proclamation, which freed slaves in

60
00:05:46.524 --> 00:05:54.248
the Confederacy. You also gave one of the most famous speeches in history: the

61
00:05:54.249 --> 00:06:01.197
Gettysburg Address. ABRAHAM LINCOLN: Ah, yes. In my Gettysburg Address, I made it clear the war was about liberty and

62
00:06:01.198 --> 00:06:07.938
freedom for all. I'm told people loved the speech. Perhaps it even contributed to my reelection

63
00:06:07.939 --> 00:06:12.926
in 1864. Unfortunately, Faye, the war was still going on when I started my second

64
00:06:12.927 --> 00:06:21.922
term. Congress resolved the slavery issue in 1865 when they drafted and passed the Thirteenth Amendment to the U.S. Constitution, abolishing slavery.

65
00:06:21.923 --> 00:06:27.418
I hadn't supported full abolition in the past, but I did support this amendment.

66
00:06:27.419 --> 00:06:36.093
Later that year Confederate General Lee surrendered to General Grant in Appomattox, Virginia, officially ending the war. FAYE

67
00:06:36.094 --> 00:06:41.285
MOSS: You must have been relieved that the country could be one union again.

68
00:06:41.286 --> 00:06:47.663
ABRAHAM LINCOLN: Yes, but it wasn't so simple. I knew I needed to work hard to rebuild the

69
00:06:47.664 --> 00:06:55.054
country. Many Americans wanted to punish the South, but I was forgiving since it was important to keep the country together.

70
00:06:55.055 --> 00:07:03.126
There were people with strong feelings on both sides, and that wasn't going away any time soon, if ever. FAYE

71
00:07:03.127 --> 00:07:06.508
MOSS: I think you're right about that, President

72
00:07:06.509 --> 00:07:12.539
Lincoln. Thank you so much for joining us. We'll let you get back to history now.

73
00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:16.445
ABRAHAM

74
00:07:16.446 --> 00:07:24.587
LINCOLN: Good-bye! FAYE MOSS: Five days after General Lee surrendered, Abraham Lincoln attended a play in Washington DC

75
00:07:24.588 --> 00:07:33.324
with his wife. He sat in a private box in Ford's Theater, where he was shot by Confederate sympathizer John

76
00:07:33.325 --> 00:07:41.059
Wilkes Booth. President Abraham Lincoln died the next morning on April

77
00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:50.461
15, 1865. Abraham Lincoln is remembered for the roles he played in abolishing slavery and preserving the U.S. as a democracy and one

78
00:07:50.462 --> 00:07:57.423
nation. Many see "Honest Abe" as a hero—no wonder his face is on the U.S. penny!

79
00:07:57.424 --> 00:08:06.930
Thanks for tuning in today. I'll be back again soon with another episode of Superstars in History !

